# Complete themes: ggtheme

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:giggly, path: Path.join(__DIR__, "..")},
  {:kino, "~> 0.19"}
  ])

alias Giggly.Plot

mtcars = Giggly.Datasets.mtcars()

render = fn plot ->
  plot
  |> Giggly.to_svg()
  |> IO.iodata_to_binary()
  |> Kino.Image.new(:svg)
end
```

## About this notebook

This notebook follows the examples on ggplot2's reference page for
[`theme_grey()`](https://ggplot2.tidyverse.org/reference/ggtheme.html), which covers
`theme_gray()`, `theme_bw()`, `theme_linedraw()`, `theme_light()`, `theme_dark()`,
`theme_minimal()`, `theme_classic()`, `theme_void()` and `theme_test()` too, in the order the page
gives them. Each example shows ggplot2's code first and then Giggly's. An example Giggly cannot
draw yet keeps its ggplot2 code and names what it is waiting for, so the page is also a list of
what remains.

A complete theme sets how everything in a plot that is not data is drawn: backgrounds, gridlines,
axes, text, legends and strips. `theme_gray()`, also spelt `theme_grey()`, is ggplot2's default: a
grey panel with white gridlines. The others vary it, from `theme_bw()`'s dark lines on white,
through `theme_classic()`'s axis lines without gridlines, to `theme_void()`, which draws nothing but
the data; `theme_test()` is meant for visual tests and should not change. Each takes a base font
size and family, base sizes for lines and rectangles, and ink, paper and accent colours. `theme()`
adjusts a theme rather than replacing it. Giggly has each of them but `theme_test()`, spelt one way,
as `Giggly.Plot.theme_gray/2`, `Giggly.Plot.theme_bw/2` and the rest, each taking `base_size:`,
`base_family:`, `header_family:`, `base_line_size:` and `base_rect_size:`, but no ink, paper or
accent colours (`ink`, `paper`, `accent`). A plot that chooses none is drawn in `theme_gray()`, and
`Giggly.Plot.theme/2` adjusts a theme. A plot is described with `Giggly.Plot` and drawn with
`Giggly.to_svg/2`, which returns an SVG document. The setup cell aliases `Giggly.Plot`, so the cells
write `Plot.new/2`, and defines `render`, which draws a plot and shows it below the cell with
`Kino.Image`. The data is `Giggly.Datasets.mtcars/0`, R's `mtcars`: 32 cars from the 1974 *Motor
Trend* US magazine, which the setup cell binds to `mtcars`, R's name for it.

## The default theme

R's `within()` turns `vs`, `am`, `cyl` and `gear` into factors, giving `vs` and `am` words for
their values.

```r
mtcars2 <- within(mtcars, {
  vs <- factor(vs, labels = c("V-shaped", "Straight"))
  am <- factor(am, labels = c("Automatic", "Manual"))
  cyl  <- factor(cyl)
  gear <- factor(gear)
})

p1 <- ggplot(mtcars2) +
  geom_point(aes(x = wt, y = mpg, colour = gear)) +
  labs(
    title = "Fuel economy declines as weight increases",
    subtitle = "(1973-74)",
    caption = "Data from the 1974 Motor Trend US magazine.",
    tag = "Figure 1",
    x = "Weight (1000 lbs)",
    y = "Fuel economy (mpg)",
    colour = "Gears"
  )

p1 + theme_gray() # the default
```

The cell makes `mtcars2` as `within()` does, each factor's values as text: `vs` and `am` as their
words, and `cyl` and `gear` as their numbers written out, so `gear` is discrete and its legend lists
3, 4 and 5. `Giggly.Plot.theme_gray/2` draws the plot in `theme_gray()`, which is Giggly's default
too.

```elixir
words = %{vs: %{0.0 => "V-shaped", 1.0 => "Straight"}, am: %{0.0 => "Automatic", 1.0 => "Manual"}}

mtcars2 =
  Enum.map(mtcars, fn
    {column, values} when column in [:vs, :am] ->
      {column, Enum.map(values, fn value -> words[column][value] end)}

    {column, values} when column in [:cyl, :gear] ->
      {column, Enum.map(values, fn value -> Integer.to_string(round(value)) end)}

    unchanged ->
      unchanged
  end)

p1 =
  mtcars2
  |> Plot.new()
  |> Plot.geom_point(%{x: :wt, y: :mpg, colour: :gear})
  |> Plot.labs(
    title: "Fuel economy declines as weight increases",
    subtitle: "(1973-74)",
    caption: "Data from the 1974 Motor Trend US magazine.",
    tag: "Figure 1",
    x: "Weight (1000 lbs)",
    y: "Fuel economy (mpg)",
        colour: "Gears"
  )

p1
|> Plot.theme_gray()
|> render.()
```

## Other complete themes

```r
p1 + theme_bw()
p1 + theme_linedraw()
p1 + theme_light()
p1 + theme_dark()
p1 + theme_minimal()
p1 + theme_classic()
p1 + theme_void()
```

```elixir
p1
|> Plot.theme_bw()
|> render.()
```

```elixir
p1
|> Plot.theme_linedraw()
|> render.()
```

```elixir
p1
